's dirt therefore has an expiry measured in seconds and set by a hand you are not holding.** ⚠️ **AND THE SHARPER HALF, which is F-2358-1's lesson landing on a HANDOFF rather than a selector: this test was written in a handoff — the last act of a fire, under budget, about a situation its author could no longer re-measure — so it is exactly the clause nobody had to defend. **A handoff instruction is a MEASUREMENT WITH A TIMESTAMP, and the shorter its subject's half-life the sooner it becomes a confident statement about a world that has moved. Re-derive an inherited test's ANTECEDENTS before you act on its CONCLUSION** (Mistake #4, applied to the test itself rather than to the claim it carries).
137:🔀 **F-2656-1 — IN A PAIRED POSITIVE/NEGATIVE CONTROL, *AGREEMENT BETWEEN THE ARMS* IS ITSELF THE BROKEN-INSTRUMENT SIGNAL — YOU NEED NOT KNOW WHICH ARM IS RIGHT (measured s2656, LIVE ON MYSELF, on the RT-01 salt check).** F-2609-1 makes the salt check step 0 of any mint, and its whole design is a pair: re-mint a banked rotation with the REAL salt (must reproduce) and with a WRONG one (must not). 📊 **MEASURED: my first probe returned `0/NaN seeds reproduce | id false | opensAt false | closesAt false` for the REAL salt AND for a deliberately wrong salt — identical verdicts on arms that are *built to disagree*.** The salt was fine; my probe had guessed the output shape, assuming a bare rotation object with an ARRAY of seeds where `rotation-mint.mjs` emits a `{rotations:[…]}` WRAPPER with `seeds` as an OBJECT keyed by contract id. Corrected against the real shape, the same subjects read **REAL `r2026w37` 6/6 · `r2026w38` 6/6 · WRONG `r2026w38` 0/6**, reproducing F-2609-1’s figures to the digit. ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, caught in ONE step, NOT a false green, and no verdict was published — the salt IS correct and tomorrow’s RT-01 mint is de-risked a day early.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is the DIRECTION had the shape error been subtler: a probe that zeroed only the seed comparison would have read `REAL 0/6` and been believed, publishing *"the owner’s salt is WRONG"* two days before a mint — a false alarm about the one input this law forbids reading directly, whose prescribed response is an expensive desk STOP. A broken probe fails toward ALARM here, not toward silence.** 🔑 **AND A SECOND, SEPARATELY MEASURED HALF THAT SHARPENS F-2609-1’s OWN WORDING: THE METADATA IS SALT-INDEPENDENT. `id`, `opensAt` and `closesAt` derive from the WEEK, not the salt — measured, the WRONG-salt arm scored `id+opensAt+closesAt identical: TRUE` while its seeds scored `0/6`.** So a comparison that folds metadata in makes a genuinely failing check look partly right, and a fire that eyeballs *"id matches, the dates match"* gets a FALSE CONFIRMATION on a secret it cannot otherwise inspect. **F-2609-1 already says compare its SEEDS; this measures why that word is load-bearing — key the comparison on seeds ALONE.** ⛔ **AND A THIRD MEASURED HALF THAT SHARPENS IT AGAIN, TAKEN FROM THE CODE RATHER THAN FROM OBSERVATION: `rotation-mint.mjs:38-41` shows `salt` enters ONLY the `createHmac`, while `id`/`opensAt`/`closesAt` derive from the ISO week — so the metadata is salt-independent BY CONSTRUCTION. And a seed is `${prefix}-${id}-${digest}` where only the trailing 12 hex chars are the HMAC: MEASURED, `131` of `203` seed characters (**64.5%**) are IDENTICAL under a wrong salt (real `e1-the-claim-r2026w38-f94c101b7c67` vs wrong `e1-the-claim-r2026w38-39591ca84e20`, 22 of 34 leading chars shared), while whole-string equality correctly reads `0 of 6`. **So "compare the seeds" must mean STRING EQUALITY IN CODE — a fire scanning seeds by eye sees a familiar prefix and a matching week and stops reading before the only 12 characters that discriminate.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: *"did a fire’s ad-hoc probe assume the right output shape?"* is unobservable to every battery — the same reason F-2567-1 declined one for a hand-typed clock, F-1669-1 for a filename read with the eyes and F-2599-1 for a `| tail`. The cure is the sentence you are reading, plus the note now standing at the RT-01 site.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is the rung BETWEEN this streak’s two newest instrument findings: F-2215-1 says assert your control PRODUCED something; F-2655-2 (one fire old) says two broken probes can corroborate each other into a confident false verdict, and warns that the standing defence — a second instrument — fails when both are yours. THIS IS THE CHEAP TELL THAT CLOSES THAT GAP: when your test already HAS an arm built to fail, you do not need a third instrument and you do not need to know which arm is right — **if the arms AGREE, the instrument is broken, whatever they agree on.** It costs nothing, it fires BEFORE you interpret either number, and it works even when you have misunderstood the subject completely. Build the negative arm not only to validate the discriminator, but to catch yourself.**
