226:💽 **F-2644-1 — THE DATA VOLUME IS AT 100% AND `ENOSPC` ACTUALLY FIRED TWICE DURING THIS FIRE, WHILE TWO PAID ASTRA RUNS WERE WRITING (measured s2644).** 📊 **MEASURED, not inferred: `df -k /System/Volumes/Data` reads `3721.9 GiB size · 3664.2 used · **28.84 GiB avail** · 100% capacity`. Two of my own tool-output writes to `/private/tmp` failed `ENOSPC: no space left on device` at 21:48 and then SUCCEEDED again minutes later — so the volume is riding the purgeable-space edge, not merely "nearly full".** 🎯 **WHAT IS NEW TODAY IS NAMED, AND THE ATTRIBUTION IS DELIBERATELY WEAKER THAN MY OWN FIRST DRAFT — I WROTE "THE PROXIMATE CONSUMER" AND THEN MEASURED THE DENOMINATOR AND CORRECTED MYSELF BEFORE PUBLISHING. The volume carries `3,664.2` GiB of USED space; this repo alone is **`500.8` GiB** by `du -sk` (13.7% of it), and the remaining ~3.16 TiB sits OUTSIDE anything this factory can see. **So the two trees below are `0.9%` of what is used and are in NO sense why the disk is full.** What they ARE is the recent DELTA: created TODAY, they hold `31.98` GiB against `28.84` GiB free, i.e. **they roughly HALVED the remaining headroom** — which is why the ENOSPC landed today and not last week. That is a claim about MARGIN, not about cause, and the two must not be confused: `scratchpad/wt-rul-county` and `scratchpad/wt-rul-play` — the two worktrees the attended session created to execute `tasks/rulings-county-2026-09-19.md` and `tasks/rulings-play-2026-09-19.md` — measure **`16,768,632` KB = 15.99 GiB EACH by `du -sk`, byte-identical to each other, `31.98` GiB combined, against `28.84` GiB free.** Neither carries a `node_modules` symlink, so F-2607-1's double-counting route is EXCLUDED by measurement rather than assumed.** ⚖️ **THE CAVEAT IS STATED RATHER THAN GLOSSED, because it bounds the claim: `du` counts ALLOCATED BLOCKS, and on APFS a tree created by clone (`cp -c`) shares blocks with its source, which `du` would count in BOTH trees. So `31.98` GiB is an UPPER BOUND on the incremental cost, not a measured unique footprint. What is NOT in doubt is the volume reading (100%, 28.84 GiB) and the ENOSPC, both taken directly.** ⚠️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost so far is ZERO — nothing was lost, no run failed, both ENOSPC hits were my own tool-output writes and both recovered, and the census independently reads these two trees as `could-not-answer / HOLDING` (23,079 evidence file(s), 9,626.2 MB each) rather than as damaged.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is the DIRECTION and the COMPANY IT KEEPS: lane-a (`sol-open-findings-astra`, 2h37m) and lane-c (`sol-map-art-campaign-2b`, 8 min) were BOTH actively writing when I measured (run logs 0.5 and 0.0 min old), and these are OWNER-FUNDED Astra runs producing art evidence. A write that fails on a full disk does not announce itself the way a red gate does — it truncates. This is Mistake #11's axis inverted: not "what dies with this disk", but "what never reaches it".** 🚫 **NOT A FIRE'S ACT, AND THE RESTRAINT IS THE LAW'S OWN: both trees are ATTENDED-OWNED (the registry gained `wt-rul-county` during this very fire), `rm` prompts by design (§4.10), and the RETENTION LAW forbids deleting untracked history. A fire REPORTS this; it does not reclaim it.** ➡️ **RECOMMENDATION, and it costs the owner nothing to wait: the two scratchpads are TRANSIENT BY CONSTRUCTION — they exist to execute the ruling and their work is drained attended, so the ~32 GiB returns when the two implementers land. If the implementers are done, removing those two trees is the whole remedy. If they are NOT done, the honest reading is that the machine has no headroom for a third such tree, and the next one should wait rather than race the Astra runs for the last 28 GiB.** ⓘ **AND `/private/tmp` IS REAPED ON THIS MACHINE (F-2607-1, measured), so these trees also have an automatic fuse measured in days — which is a reason NOT to hand-delete them and a reason the attended session should land their work rather than leave it to the reaper.** ⏱️ **AMENDED s2644, TWELVE MINUTES AFTER I PUBLISHED IT, BY THE FIRE THAT WROTE IT — THE HEADLINE NUMBER IS TRUE AND *NOT REPEATABLE*, AND A READER ACTING ON IT TOMORROW WOULD BE WRONG BY 24x. RESTATED, NOT DELETED, BECAUSE THE SEQUENCE IS THE FINDING.** 📊 **RE-MEASURED at 22:00, the same command on the same volume: `3.6 TiB size · **2.9 TiB used** · **692 GiB avail** · 82% capacity` — against the `3664.2 GiB used / 28.84 GiB avail / 100%` I measured at 21:48. **~663 GiB returned in twelve minutes.**** ⛔ **AND IT WAS NOT THE TWO SCRATCHPADS, which is the half I checked rather than assumed: `wt-rul-county` and `wt-rul-play` BOTH STILL EXIST on disk at 22:00, unchanged. So the `31.98` GiB I named as the recent delta was never reclaimed, and cannot account for a 663 GiB swing.** ⓘ **THE MECHANISM IS UNMEASURED AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT GUESSED (F-2182-1): the shape is consistent with macOS reclaiming APFS purgeable space (local snapshots, caches) under the pressure of hitting 100%, which is the documented behaviour and is why "Avail" and "Capacity" disagree on this filesystem — but I did not measure a snapshot list, so that is an INFERENCE and is labelled one. What is MEASURED is the before, the after, and that the named trees survived both.** ⚖️ **WHAT SURVIVES UNREFUTED, AND IT IS THE WHOLE POINT: the volume REALLY DID reach 100% with 28.84 GiB available, and `ENOSPC` REALLY DID fire twice on ordinary tool-output writes. Both are direct observations, neither is retracted, and the machine really did refuse to write for a fire that was doing nothing unusual.** 🎯 **WHAT CHANGES IS THE URGENCY AND THEREFORE THE RECOMMENDATION: this is a SELF-HEALING condition, not a standing shortage, so the "no headroom for a third tree" reading above is RETIRED — at 692 GiB there is ample headroom today, and I gated a drain on this machine minutes later precisely because the re-measurement said I safely could. The condition remains worth knowing because it is RECURRING BY CONSTRUCTION: a 500.8 GiB repo on a volume that must purge to make room will reach that wall again, and the next purge may free less.** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF IS F-2601-1's LESSON ON A NEW SUBJECT, AND IT COST ME TWELVE MINUTES TO LEARN TWICE: that finding showed a `systemctl` `NEXT` column is a DRAW FROM A DISTRIBUTION banked as a constant. **A FREE-SPACE READING ON AN APFS VOLUME IS THE SAME SHAPE** — it is a sample of a system that actively reclaims, so "28.84 GiB free" is not a property of the disk, it is a property of the disk AT THAT INSTANT UNDER THAT PRESSURE. When you bank a resource measurement, bank the TIMESTAMP and re-take it before anyone acts on it; and when the resource is one the OS manages elastically, say so, because the next reader will otherwise treat your sample as a constraint.** ⚠️ **AND THE METHOD NOTE THAT ACTUALLY CAUGHT IT: I did not re-measure out of diligence. I re-measured because I was about to gate a large drain and wanted to know whether the battery's output would fit — i.e. **the check that found my own error was a check I ran for an unrelated reason.** A published figure nobody has cause to re-take is a figure that stays wrong.**
