266:🔺 **F-2640-1 — `health-watch`’S `fire :` ROW IS STRUCTURALLY INCAPABLE OF SEEING THE FIRE THAT READS IT: `pgrep` DOES NOT REPORT ITS OWN CALLER’S ANCESTORS, SO THE ONE PANEL §2.0c MAKES A BINDING TRIAGE READ SAID `between-fires` ON EVERY FIRE, FOREVER, WHILE ONE WAS LIVE (measured and cured s2640, dry board, 105th consecutive; CODEX-WALL standing, owner POLICY, read AT SOURCE).** `fire_proc()` is `pgrep -f "claude -p # Gold Rush FIRE"`, and §2.0c makes `health-watch.sh status` a duty on every fire while §2.0d routes it through `node` — so the fire is ALWAYS an ancestor of the pgrep it spawns. 📊 **MEASURED s2640, the control asserting its own validity FIRST (F-2215-1): `pgrep -f .` returned `1062` pids and **ZERO of the caller’s four ancestors**, while two NON-ancestor controls of the SAME `comm` (`/bin/zsh`, `bash`) were VISIBLE — including `60040`, a `/bin/zsh` spawned by my own fire, so the discriminator is ancestry and not binary, user or session.** 🔬 **THE VARIABLES WERE SEPARATED RATHER THAN ASSUMED, which is what makes the scope argument hold: the fire’s own argv is **973,203 B** (the whole of `fire.md` in one element), so SIZE was the obvious competing explanation — but a non-ancestor child carrying a **900,094-byte** argv was matched by `pgrep -f` at BOTH the start AND the very END of it, while a **72-byte** ancestor (`fire-runner.sh`) is invisible. Neither argv size nor match position explains it; **ancestry alone is sufficient**.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, nothing is broken, and this is NOT a false green about work. `status` mode prints the panel and EXITS at `health-watch.sh:191`, BEFORE check 3 — so a fire-run panel writes NO alert, sends NO notification and changes NO state. The defect was confined to ONE DISPLAY ROW.** ⛔ **AND THE OBVIOUS ALARMING READING IS REFUTED RATHER THAN LEFT HANGING: `logs/health.log` holds **89** `Fire lock looks DEAD` alerts — **71% of all 125 alerts ever raised** — and they are NOT false positives from this cause. They carry the `[health]` prefix, i.e. the launchd `watch` path, for which a fire is not an ancestor and which never calls `dashboard()`; and the 900 KB control above shows that observer can match a fire’s argv. **Check 3 is SOUND.**** 🎯 **What earns it a cure is the DIRECTION at the one moment it fires: §2.0c cost 37h50m across 35 fires to establish that a fire MUST read this panel, and the panel’s `fire :` row was the one line guaranteed wrong for its prescribed reader — stating, affirmatively, that no fire is running at the exact moment one is. That fails toward “the lock is unowned”, the lock-stealing direction §1.1 exists to prevent. The cost is zero only because §1.1 binds on the `FIRE END` log signal rather than on this row.** 🛠️ **CURED AT THE SITE so it cannot rot out of sync with prose: the row now DECLARES and does NOT refuse (F-2366-1’s choice for BUSY) — exit codes untouched — keying on `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, the house fire-shell discriminator `playwright.config.ts`’s `isFireShell` already uses (§3.1, guarded by `fire-shell-serialisation.test.mjs`): `fire   : RUNNING (you, the caller) — pgrep cannot see its own ancestors…`. **GATED, never always-on**: an unconditional line names a blind spot an idle board does not have, which is how a declaration decays into a formality.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/health-watch-fire-line-guard.test.mjs` — 7 arms, 47 ms, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`; `gate-caller-audit` PASS. EXTRACTION-based (the `main-lock-gate-guard` house pattern) because running the real `dashboard()` fires three `curl` edge probes at up to 10 s each, and it REFUSES rather than passing vacuously if its subject block is gone (F-2217-1).** 🔬 **TEETH, 9 variants manufactured IN PLACE with the subject restored BYTE-IDENTICALLY (sha256-asserted) after every one: ALL 7 ARMS red under at least one SUBSTANTIVE variant, so none is decoration — over-general always-on reds **3 and 5** (exactly the two reverse controls built for it) · empty-discriminator-counts-as-present reds **5 ALONE** · dropping the finding id reds **1 ALONE** · dropping the mechanism word reds **2 ALONE** · stripping provenance reds **6 ALONE** · breaking the `status` dispatch reds **7 ALONE** · inverting the visible-fire branch reds **4 ALONE**.** ⚠️ **REPORTED HONESTLY RATHER THAN AS COVERAGE: the PRE-CURE and TOTAL-FAILURE variants each red FIVE arms, but by CONSTRUCTION REFUSAL — the extraction cannot find its subject, so every arm that evaluates it refuses at once. That is the guard failing LOUD, which is correct, and it is NOT evidence that arms 3 and 4 detect the pre-cure defect (F-2477-1). Their teeth come from V2 and V8.** ⚠️ **THREE METHOD NOTES PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE, and two of them are my own errors caught by controls rather than by reading. (1) I TRUNCATED MY OWN CENSUS AT 6 and read “pgrep sees the attended claudes but not mine” off a `.slice(0,6)` — the F-2606-1 truncation trap, committed while measuring. The membership test (`list.includes(pid)`) over the UNSLICED output is what actually answered it. (2) MY FIRST TWO ARGV-SIZE EXPERIMENTS WERE VOID AND BOTH LOOKED LIKE FINDINGS: `sleep 8 <pad>` rejects a second operand so the subject never existed (`psArgvBytes=0`), and `sh -c "sleep 8" <pad>` EXEC’D THE PADDING AWAY (argv collapsed to 8 B). Both would have read as “pgrep cannot match long argvs”. **Asserting the subject was ALIVE and that its argv was RETAINED is what separated a void arm from a result.** (3) `launchctl getenv CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` returned EMPTY — and so did a `PATH` control, so that probe CANNOT ANSWER and is not claimed. The cure’s safety does not rest on it: the launchd agent runs with NO argument and never reaches `dashboard()`.** 🚫 **NO GUARD OVER THE PROCESS TREE, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: “did a fire’s pgrep see its own ancestor?” is a runtime property of the process tree, unobservable to every battery — the same reason F-1669-1 declined one for a filename read with the eyes and F-2567-1 for a hand-typed clock — and a red on `fire_proc == false` would fire during every legitimate between-fires launchd pass, i.e. during ordinary correct operation, and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate).** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS after *how WIDE is the selector* (F-2358-1), *can its axis EXPRESS the defect* (F-2389-1), *which TREE* (F-2484-1), *a registry is not a census* (F-2485-1), *entries vs files* (F-2490-1), *an undeclared denominator* (F-2561-1), *which CLOCK* (F-2567-1) and *a refusal is a bucket* (F-2617-1): every one of those asks whether the SUBJECT SET was drawn correctly. **THIS ONE HAS A PERFECT SUBJECT SET THAT STRUCTURALLY EXCLUDES THE OBSERVER.** A liveness probe is the one instrument that cannot answer *“am I running?”*, because the act of asking creates the ancestry that hides the answer — and the miss is SILENT and reads as good news. **When an instrument reports on a population, ask whether the READER is a member of it, and whether the act of reading removes them.**
291:🔺 **F-2630-1 — THE F-2628-1 WIDENING IS PRICED AND **REJECTED BY MEASUREMENT**: ADMITTING ON A ROW’S OWN “OWNER’S DESK” WORDS RUNS AT A **66.7% KEY-DRIFT FALSE-POSITIVE FLOOR** — ~2.9× THE s1533 FIGURE s2628 CITED AS ITS REASON FOR DECLINING (measured s2630).** s2628 left this priced and unclaimed with an explicit condition: *“if you take it, run the s1533 pricing over the same corpus FIRST; do not widen on the strength of my one instance.”* **I RAN IT.** 🔬 **METHOD — DRIVE THE GUARD’S OWN EXPORTS, NEVER REIMPLEMENT THEM**, which is s2348’s correction of s2347 (both of whose errors came from a private reimplementation of `rowId()` and of desk membership): the replay imports `addedRows` / `rowId` / `gateOf` / `isOwnerGate` / `declaresOwnerAct` / `closureMarked` / `deskTail` / `carriesId` / `notOwed` / `isLockLine` from `scripts/desk-birth-guard.mjs` and `DESK_WORD` from `scripts/desk-declaration-guard.mjs`, and selects windows by F-2260-1’s identity rule (consecutive same-session handoff commits collapsed to that session’s FINAL one, or the window collapses onto the fire’s own earlier handoff). Corpus: **1,921 session windows reaching `kind:”window”`** of 2,448 distinct handoff sessions. ✅ **CONTROL ASSERTED FIRST (F-2215-1), AND ITS DIVERGENCE IS REPORTED HONESTLY RATHER THAN HIDDEN: the decisive banked figure REPRODUCES — the shipped `act` arm yields exactly `1` FAIL verdict and it is `F-MAIL-0829` at `s2346`, s2348’s banked result to the digit.** Its ADMISSION count reads `3` against the banked `1`, and that is **corpus growth, verified not assumed**: the two extras are `s2386:F-2386-1` and `s2523:F-2473-1`, both post-dating s2348 by 38 and 175 fires (`f7dfd5d48`, `e666f83fd`), and **neither produces a verdict — both were correctly desked.** ⚠️ **MY CONTROL PREDICATE WAS THE THING THAT WAS WRONG, NOT THE HARNESS: I tested strict equality on a figure that is DESIGNED TO GROW, so it printed `CONTROL DIVERGES` on a harness that was working. A control over a living corpus must assert the INVARIANT (the verdict set) and not the TALLY.** 📊 **THE RESULT: the naive widening admits `86` new rows and produces **`24` NEW FAIL VERDICTS**, with `89` further rows suppressed by the existing closure filter. Judged by the s1533 key-drift test — is the desk phrase about THIS row’s thread, or about a DIFFERENT finding? — **`16` of `24` name another thread entirely: `66.7%`.** Nearest-preceding-id subjects on the drifted rows are other people’s threads outright (`s1196:F-1196-1` → F-1167-3 · `s1532:F-1532-1` → F-1167-1 · `s2071:F-2071-1` → F-1815-1 · `s2168:F-2168-1` → F-2165-1 · `s2450:F-PT16-1` → F-PT16-2), and one admission (`s1499`) keys on the slug **`building`**, which is not an id at all. ⬇️ **AND `66.7%` IS A FLOOR, NOT A POINT ESTIMATE, BECAUSE MY PROXY OVER-CREDITS: `s1333:F-1333-3` scores SELF only because its own header is the nearest preceding id token — the SENTENCE’s subject is one of s1332’s findings (*“S1332’S TWO FINDINGS … ONE OF THEM IS ON THE OWNER’S DESK”*), so it is key drift my diagnostic cannot see. Read strictly, true drift is ≥ 17/24.** 🎯 **AND THE SELF-REFERENTIAL REMAINDER DOES NOT RESCUE IT: of the `8` scored SELF, `F-1055-2` (*“on the OWNER’S DESK s1055”*) and `F-2432-3` (*“IT IS OWNER DESK ITEM (ii) RECURRING”*) are rows RECORDING that a thread is ALREADY desked — precisely the shape F-2259-1 replayed at 3-new-verdicts-ALL-FALSE and rejected, because **this guard’s remedy MANUFACTURES desk items out of already-handled threads.** A self-reference-tightened arm therefore still prices at roughly `37–62%` false positives, against the `0 in 15` this guard’s own pricing table chose and the `0` the shipped `act` arm holds. ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: nothing is broken, nothing was lost, realised cost ZERO, and this is NOT a false green — the guard is behaving exactly as documented and every number it has printed was true. s2628’s finding STANDS unrefuted: the population it named is real, and `F-HEAT14-3` really did sit on no desk.** ✅ **WHAT THIS CHANGES IS ONLY THE DISPOSITION: the widening is now a MEASURED NO rather than an open question, and s2628’s (C) is DISCHARGED. ⛔ DO NOT RE-TAKE IT — neither the naive form nor the self-reference-tightened form clears this guard’s own false-positive bar, and the pricing costs a fire’s budget to re-derive.** 🔑 **THE RECOVERABLE SIGNAL, NAMED SO THE NEXT READER INHERITS A LEAD AND NOT A DEAD END: `3` of the 24 are unambiguous genuine misses that declare themselves in their OWN words and name no other thread — `F-1522-1` (*“[OWNER’S DESK — the cure touches the runner Robin runs in his own Terminal]”*), `F-E2PL-1` (*“NOT yet on the OWNER’S DESK because a fire may not edit STATUS line 1 — owed to the next attended window”*) and `F-HEAT14-3` itself. **All three are self-declaring and none is a closure record.** A predicate keyed on that narrower shape might clear the bar; it is UNMEASURED and I do not claim it does. 🚫 **NO CURE SHIPPED AND NO GUARD CHANGED, and the restraint is now MEASURED rather than inherited — which is the whole point of running the pricing: s2628 declined on a cited 23%, and the real figure for this corpus is 66.7% or worse.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS after *how WIDE is the selector* (F-2358-1) and *can its axis EXPRESS the defect* (F-2389-1): **A VOCABULARY IS NOT A SUBJECT.** A row’s text is a whole narrative, and this factory’s house style QUOTES other findings constantly — so a predicate keyed on a PHRASE selects every row that MENTIONS the concept, not the rows that ARE an instance of it. The tighter `GATE:`/`owner action` keys work not because they are rarer but because they are **STRUCTURAL**: they occupy a declared position in the row rather than appearing anywhere in its prose. When you widen a selector from a STRUCTURE to a PHRASE, price the drift before you ship it — in a corpus that cites itself, the phrase belongs to somebody else two times in three.
